<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-web-hosting">
  <title>GitHub: Web Hosting</title>
<introduction>
  <p>
    When developing a game, or any important software, it's important to have
    a backup of your work that is located separately from your original work.
    In addition, if your game has multiple authors, then this separate location
    can act as a place to share code with your collaborators.
    A service that is often used for this is Cloud Network Storage. Examples are
    Dropbox, Microsoft OneDrive, and Apple iCloud. These services automate the backup
    process using a revision control system that is similar to git along with some
    automation tasks so that checkpoints (a.k.a. commits) happen at regular intervals
    or when certain events happen such as saving a file. This automated checkpointing
    can interfere with some integrated development environments because some large 
    files change frequently, such as compiled binaries. Many of these files are
    derived from the source files, so they should not be continually backed up.
    In addition, if multiple people are editing a file in different locations,
    then the changes can sometimes overwrite each other or multiple versions of the 
    file will be put on the server.
  </p>
  <p>
    Web Hosting for version control systems such as git, mercurial, and subversion
    give users the responsibility for when backups are created, which types of files will
    be backed up and how to merge changes when files have been modified by multiple
    users or in multiple locations. They do this by delegating the backup process 
    to that of the version control system. This book describes and uses GitHub for web hosting. Other 
    web hosting for code include BitBucket, Codeberg, GitLab, and SourceForge to 
    name a few. Each has its own features and fee structure, and all have a free
    to use option for public repositories.
  </p>
  <p>
    GitHub uses git for repository storage. Automation is done through GitHub
    actions which are composed using .yaml (YAML Ain't Markup Language) files.
    GitHub also uses OAuth, which means that you can use your GitHub id to 
    authenticate on other sites that have added GitHub as an authentication 
    provider.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rest of this section will talk about GitHub Repositories and Setting up
    your account on GitHub, and the basics of git.
  </p>
  </introduction>
  <subsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-web-hosting-repositories">
    <title>GitHub Repositories</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>
        In its simplest usage, you can think of a GitHub repository as a web interface
        to files stored online that keep track of changes to files uploaded to the 
        same exact path. With this type of usage, you can have files locally
        in any location, and have them organized differently on GitHub. This usage
        will keep your code backed up, but it can make keeping your files in sync
        more difficult than it needs to be.
    </p>
    <p>
        The intended usage is that after creating a GitHub repository, you clone
        the repository to your computer on a local drive (not a mounted cloud drive)
        in a folder that is not part of an automatic backup system, since you'll
        be controlling the backups of the repository using git.
    </p>
</introduction>
<subsubsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-web-hosting-repositories-tools">
<title>Tools</title>
<p>
    There are multiple tools that can be used for syncing your local repository with the web-hosted 
    version of the repository. <url href="https://desktop.github.com/download/">GitHub Desktop</url>
    is a Graphical User Interface (GUI) for managing your local repositories. There is also
    a command line option. The easiest command line option is to use the 
    <url href="https://cli.github.com">GitHub CLI (gh)</url> along with <url href="https://git-scm.com">git</url>.
    When you install GitHub Desktop, it will install a version of git for you, but you'll need to install
    git and the GitHub CLI (gh) separately in order to do all of the git tasks. It is possible
    to only use git to interface with GitHub on the command line, but authentication is much
    simpler using gh rather than git.
</p>
</subsubsection>
<subsubsection xml:id="infrastructure-introduction-github-web-hosting-repositories-work-flow">
<title>Basic Work Flow</title>
<p>
    There are a few steps that are important to remember when using git for version control.
    First, when you don't yet have a local copy of the repository, the first thing you need 
    to do is <term>clone</term> the repository to a local folder. You only need to do this
    step (clone) once per computer that you are working on. Once you clone a repository, 
    there are different options you can do from inside the repository. You can <term>pull</term>
    changes from GitHub (the remote repository). You can <term>add</term> files for commit that 
    have been created or modified. You can <term>commit</term> the added files with a message
    describing the changes. You can <term>push</term> the committed changes back to 
    GitHub (the remote repository).
</p>
<p>
    The typical order of working in a local repository is to 
    <ol>
        <li><term>pull</term> changes from GitHub before making any changes.</li>
        <li>Modify or add any files that you are working on.</li>
        <li>Make sure that your programs still work or are in better shape than they were.</li>
        <li><term>add</term> files for commit that have changes that you want to keep.</li>
        <li><term>commit</term> files with a message describing the changes.</li>
        <li><term>push</term> the changes back to GitHub.</li>
    </ol>
    Any time you plan to make changes to your files, you should do all of the above steps. That
    way when you start with step 1, there should be no conflicts in the files that you 
    pull from GitHub.
</p>
<p>
    This work flow can include more steps, because you may want to have multiple add and 
    commit sessions before you push. But, it is often a good idea to push changes that you 
    have committed before you take a break or stop for the day.
</p>
</subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>
